**Android has three built-in ways to show a message on the lock screen of a lost or stolen phone.** You can add a permanent line of text (`Settings > Display > Lock screen > Add text on lock screen`, or **Contact information** on a Samsung), and after the phone is gone you can send a message to it with Find Hub's **Mark as lost**. All three are plain text aimed at an honest finder. None of them react when someone tries to break in. For that, CrookCatcher can fill the whole screen with your own picture and text after a wrong PIN, or with the photo it just took of the person holding your phone.

## 1. Add a permanent text to the lock screen

This is the "If found, please call…" line. It's always visible, so it works even if the phone has no connection.

**On a Pixel and most Android phones, go to:**
`Settings > Display > Lock screen > Add text on lock screen`

On newer Pixels you can also touch and hold the home screen, then go to `Wallpaper & style > Lock screen > More lock screen settings > Add text on lock screen`.

**On a Samsung Galaxy, go to:**
`Settings > Lock screen and AOD > Contact information`

Type your text and tap **Done**. On One UI 7 and later this is where the setting lives; older versions had it in the lock screen editor.

What to write: a phone number or email address that isn't on this phone, such as a partner's number or a second email address. Keep in mind that anyone who picks up the phone can read it, so don't include your home address.

## 2. Add emergency contacts

Android can also show emergency contacts from the lock screen. A finder swipes up on the locked screen, taps **Emergency**, then **View emergency info**.

Set it up in the **Safety** app: `Your info > Emergency contacts > Add contact`. Under **Emergency info access**, check that **Show when locked** is on.

## 3. Send a message after the phone is gone: Mark as lost

If you didn't set anything up beforehand, you can still put a message on the lock screen remotely.

1. Open [google.com/android/find](https://www.google.com/android/find) on another device and sign in to your Google Account.
2. Choose your phone and tap **Mark as lost**.
3. Enter your contact information and a message to show on the lock screen.
4. Tap **Mark as lost** again.

This also locks the phone with your PIN, pattern or password. It only reaches the phone if it's switched on and online, which is why a thief's first move is often to turn it off. See [how to stop power off and airplane mode from the lock screen](/blog/block-power-menu).

## What the built-in messages can't do

These options are made for a phone that was lost and found by someone honest. Against a thief, or someone snooping, they fall short:

- **Text only.** No picture, and on most phones one small line.
- **Always the same.** The text doesn't change when someone enters a wrong PIN. Nothing tells the person that the phone noticed them.
- **Mark as lost needs a connection**, and you have to notice the phone is gone first.
- **No evidence.** You don't learn who has the phone or where it is from a lock screen message.

## Show a full-screen message after a wrong PIN

CrookCatcher's **Show message** works the other way round. Nothing is shown until someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password. Then the whole screen changes to a message you designed.

You build it in an editor inside the app:

- **Your text**, anywhere on the screen. Tap to edit, drag to move, pinch to resize. There are suggestions to start from, such as "Give me back my phone!" and "Nice try".
- **Your own picture** as the background, or a plain color. Add a picture and a caption and you have your own meme, or a jump scare for the friend who keeps guessing your PIN.
- **The crook's photo.** Instead of your picture, CrookCatcher can use the photo it just took of the person. They enter a wrong PIN, and a second later they're looking at their own face with your words on top.

That last one is the reason this feature exists. A thief who sees "Smile! You've been photographed" over their own face knows the photo already exists. At the same moment CrookCatcher emails that photo to you, with the phone's location. Leaving the phone somewhere it can be found starts to look like the better option.

